  "fibres": [],
  "measures": [],
  "notes": "Polarisation spectroscopy (Wieman and Hänsch 1976) on the rubidium D2 line, with no field. A strong pump, made circular by a quarter-wave plate, meets a weak linearly polarised probe head on in the cell. Atoms the pump talks to (one velocity class, as in saturated absorption) are saturated more for one hand of circular light than the other, so there the cell becomes birefringent and dichroic for the probe: its polarisation turns a little, only at the sub-Doppler resonances. A polarising cube at 45° to the probe (after a half-wave plate at 22.5°) splits it evenly onto two diodes; their difference is a narrow dispersive signal with no Doppler background, ready for a lock. Press Play on the laser’s sweep. Take the quarter-wave plate out (drag it away) and the signal goes. The model saturates each transition alone, with no optical pumping between levels: the positions and shapes are right, the sizes only roughly.",
  "ui": {
    "sel": "cell",
    "pins": [],
    "views": {
      "cell": {}
    }
  }
